We need to refer to the last item before removal: that is the element at index n-1 before pop. Since we remove and return it, after the call the stack size decreased by 1. The result equals the element that was at the last index before removal. The comment also includes case when that element is null: result is null and the last item in the stack is null. Actually both are same: result equals the last item (which could be null). But they phrase as either result is null and last item is null, or result equals last item. That's logically equivalent to result == \old(this.get(n-1)). However need to ensure that if result is null then old last item is null; but equality works for null as well. So assert: \old(this.size()) > 0 => (\result == null && \old(this.get(\old(this.size())-1)) == null) || (\result.equals(\old(this.get(\old(this.size())-1))))? But need to handle null equality: we can use (\result == \old(this.get(...))) which works for null reference equality. In Java, == works for reference equality, null matches null. So we can assert:

\old(this.size()) > 0 => \result == \old(this.get(\old(this.size())-1))

But the comment splits into two cases; we could mirror that:

\old(this.size()) > 0 => (\result == null && \old(this.get(\old(this.size())-1)) == null) || (\result == \old(this.get(\old(this.size())-1)));

But the first disjunct is redundant, but okay.

